# 01 — Basic Digital PLL Simulation

This notebook introduces a simple digital PLL model implemented in Python.
It demonstrates:
- Phase detector behavior
- Loop filter integration
- VCO frequency control
- Locking behavior over time

This forms the foundation for later work on injection locking and wavelet analysis.

import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

from pll.pll_model import DigitalPLL

# Simulation parameters
fs = 1e6          # simulation sampling rate (Hz)
t_end = 0.01      # total simulation time (seconds)
N = int(fs * t_end)

# Reference clock frequency
f_ref = 10e3      # 10 kHz reference

# PLL parameters
kvco = 1e3        # VCO gain (Hz per unit control voltage)
loop_gain = 0.05  # loop filter gain
pll = DigitalPLL(
    f_ref=f_ref,
    kvco=kvco,
    loop_gain=loop_gain,
    fs=fs
)
phase_error = np.zeros(N)
vco_freq = np.zeros(N)

for i in range(N):
    pe, fvco = pll.update()
    phase_error[i] = pe
    vco_freq[i] = fvco

plt.figure(figsize=(10,4))
plt.plot(phase_error)
plt.title("PLL Phase Error Over Time")
plt.xlabel("Samples")
plt.ylabel("Phase Error (radians)")
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()

plt.figure(figsize=(10,4))
plt.plot(vco_freq)
plt.title("VCO Frequency Tracking")
plt.xlabel("Samples")
plt.ylabel("Frequency (Hz)")
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()

## Interpretation

- The phase error starts high because the VCO begins at its free-running frequency.
- As the loop filter integrates the phase error, the VCO frequency adjusts.
- The VCO gradually aligns with the reference frequency.
- Once locked, the phase error stabilizes near zero.

This demonstrates correct PLL behavior and validates the basic model.
